WEBVTT

1
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:21.754
During the First World War. Time Square was
the premier theater district in the nation.

2
00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:23.849
And today. as you can
see. it's just as exciting.

3
00:00:23.882 --> 00:00:25.927
Hey. it's MTV where they filmed TRL.

4
00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:27.487
Great. Take a picture.

5
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.734
You need to be in it. too.

6
00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:30.613
You're killing me with the
pictures. You know that. right?

7
00:00:30.646 --> 00:00:32.492
We're on vacation.

8
00:00:32.505 --> 00:00:33.852
Ask that guy to take it for us.

9
00:00:33.885 --> 00:00:35.498
Hurry before we drive by.

10
00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:37.443
You want it so bad. you ask him.

11
00:00:38.109 --> 00:00:39.805
Excuse me. sir?

12
00:00:40.503 --> 00:00:41.417
Hello?

13
00:00:41.806 --> 00:00:42.787
Excuse me?

14
00:00:42.853 --> 00:00:43.884
Sir? Hello?

15
00:00:51.054 --> 00:00:52.933
How long is this tour?

16
00:00:55.144 --> 00:00:56.657
That was funny.

17
00:00:57.771 --> 00:00:59.135
So. what do we got here?

18
00:00:59.214 --> 00:01:01.725
New twist on the "Bitch Ride to Hell."

19
00:01:01.775 --> 00:01:05.998
Gang members dig up a rival's corpse and
send it around the city on the subway?

20
00:01:06.029 --> 00:01:07.808
I mean. this isn't Chicago. Mac.

21
00:01:07.841 --> 00:01:09.238
It's urban legend.

22
00:01:09.254 --> 00:01:11.266
Looks like a practical joke.

23
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:13.095
Times Square. Tour bus.

24
00:01:13.112 --> 00:01:14.542
Tourist T-shirt.

25
00:01:15.088 --> 00:01:17.798
Somebody was trying
to scare the tourists

26
00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:20.442
and used a prop shop skeleton. huh?

27
00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:26.524
Store-bought skeletons
are bleached and processed.

28
00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:29.480
They also have drill marks
from when they were assembled.

29
00:01:29.513 --> 00:01:32.190
This skull is brown and no drill marks.

30
00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:33.870
And it's missing a mandible.

31
00:01:35.446 --> 00:01:37.557
If this is a joke. I'm not laughing.

32
00:01:39.569 --> 00:01:40.783
These bones are real.

33
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:33.661
This is it?

34
00:02:33.794 --> 00:02:34.426
Yeah.

35
00:02:36.019 --> 00:02:41.290
Mannequin clothes. ball cap... bones...

36
00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:43.451
Clever.

37
00:02:43.949 --> 00:02:45.063
Not that clever.

38
00:02:45.096 --> 00:02:48.122
Whoever assembled your skeleton
never took an anatomy class.

39
00:02:48.206 --> 00:02:50.517
Humerus was where the ulna should be.

40
00:02:51.411 --> 00:02:53.855
Metacarpals were
reversed with phalanges.

41
00:02:53.905 --> 00:02:55.418
Held together by wire.

42
00:02:56.998 --> 00:02:59.591
He used this for the fingers.

43
00:02:59.923 --> 00:03:01.237
Adhesive putty.

44
00:03:03.831 --> 00:03:08.270
Looks like our "sculptor" left
a little piece of himself behind.

45
00:03:10.829 --> 00:03:11.926
There he is.

46
00:03:12.026 --> 00:03:12.591
"He"?

47
00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:13.290
Yeah.

48
00:03:14.221 --> 00:03:15.883
Pronounced brow ridge.

49
00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:18.278
Large mastoid process behind
where the ears would be.

50
00:03:18.316 --> 00:03:19.680
And this seals it.

51
00:03:20.831 --> 00:03:21.969
Inion hook.

52
00:03:21.988 --> 00:03:23.860
No doubt about it. this skull was male.

53
00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.607
Off to a good start. What else?

54
00:03:25.627 --> 00:03:28.096
Well. head is long and narrow.
Same with the nasal aperture.

55
00:03:28.115 --> 00:03:31.376
Nasal bone and spine are long
stick out over the maxilla.

56
00:03:31.395 --> 00:03:33.594
Caucasoid traits. He's white.

57
00:03:33.629 --> 00:03:39.205
How old? Medial clavicle is the last
bone to fuse. Happens between 18 and 25.

58
00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.240
A flake develops on the clavicle
where it connects to the sternum.

59
00:03:45.706 --> 00:03:50.452
It eventually envelops the entire end
cap of the clavicle. smoothing it out.

60
00:03:51.128 --> 00:03:53.076
But Look at his.

61
00:03:53.517 --> 00:03:55.176
Round and lumpy.

62
00:03:55.311 --> 00:03:56.758
Flake hasn't yet started to form.

63
00:03:56.796 --> 00:03:58.263
He wasn't even 18.

64
00:03:58.282 --> 00:03:59.845
He was just a kid.

65
00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:03.819
Homicide?

66
00:04:03.913 --> 00:04:04.878
Maybe.

67
00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:06.942
This fracture never healed.

68
00:04:06.981 --> 00:04:08.717
So. it could have been a death blow?

69
00:04:08.756 --> 00:04:10.936
Well. without the rest of the
bones. all I can do is speculate.

70
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.582
Well. tendons. tissues
and ligaments are all gone.

71
00:04:14.646 --> 00:04:16.865
What. probably three
years since he died?

72
00:04:16.903 --> 00:04:17.443
Mm-hmm.

73
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.948
Bones are in good shape.

74
00:04:18.987 --> 00:04:21.302
He was probably shielded
from Mother Nature.

75
00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:23.482
Only thing out of the
ordinary is their color.

76
00:04:23.498 --> 00:04:27.414
The natural decomposition
color of bones is brown.

77
00:04:27.453 --> 00:04:30.945
So. where did this black
discoloration come from?

78
00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:33.279
Well. that's for you
folks on the outside.

79
00:04:35.277 --> 00:04:36.994
I need to borrow a finger.

80
00:04:39.888 --> 00:04:42.782
Skeleton on a tour bus in Times Square.

81
00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:44.785
The Post is going to love this story.

82
00:04:44.823 --> 00:04:46.772
Practically writes itself.

83
00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.238
It's not news.

84
00:04:48.277 --> 00:04:50.592
True. but it's gonna sell
a hell of a lot of papers.

85
00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.482
Only because of where it was found.

86
00:04:52.493 --> 00:04:55.714
The real story is that these
are the bones of a teenage boy.

87
00:04:55.753 --> 00:04:57.296
The media don't care.

88
00:04:57.451 --> 00:04:58.435
I do.

89
00:05:03.582 --> 00:05:06.283
Mac. when can I get
my hands on that skull?

90
00:05:06.321 --> 00:05:08.173
One step at a time. Aiden.

91
00:05:08.487 --> 00:05:10.397
We have to find the rest
of his skeleton first.

92
00:05:10.417 --> 00:05:13.773
I'm just saying. intact
skulls are few and far between.

93
00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:16.301
If the time comes. I
don't want it farmed out.

94
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.535
I can handle it.

95
00:05:17.574 --> 00:05:19.233
Relax. You got it.

96
00:05:19.434 --> 00:05:21.364
I'll find you when I need you.

97
00:05:21.402 --> 00:05:22.483
Human skull.

98
00:05:23.351 --> 00:05:24.489
Big fun.

99
00:05:28.733 --> 00:05:30.720
Danny. what do you see?

100
00:05:33.091 --> 00:05:35.445
The substance isn't
just coating the bone.

101
00:05:35.522 --> 00:05:37.085
It's layered on itself.

102
00:05:37.104 --> 00:05:38.609
Means it's been there a while.

103
00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:39.959
Take a deeper sample.

104
00:05:49.542 --> 00:05:52.590
Top layer is composed
of aromatic hydrocarbons.

105
00:05:53.725 --> 00:05:54.979
Engine exhaust.

106
00:05:55.017 --> 00:05:55.905
MTBE?

107
00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.663
No. Means the engine was diesel.

108
00:05:58.683 --> 00:06:02.117
Rules out most cars. Probably
came from a truck or a bus.

109
00:06:02.151 --> 00:06:04.717
So. your boy's laying low
in a high-traffic area.

110
00:06:04.756 --> 00:06:07.225
Unfortunately. in New
York. that's everywhere.

111
00:06:14.136 --> 00:06:16.895
Deeper sample is also diesel exhaust.

112
00:06:17.416 --> 00:06:19.538
But check the benzene concentration.

113
00:06:21.294 --> 00:06:22.760
Through the roof.

114
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.655
What kind of diesel engine spits
out this much particulate matter?

115
00:06:25.674 --> 00:06:26.484
An old one.

116
00:06:26.716 --> 00:06:30.285
EPA changed diesel
emissions standards in 1994.

117
00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.944
So. if he has this much
benzene on his bones...

118
00:06:32.982 --> 00:06:35.992
He's been collecting dust a
lot longer than three years.

119
00:06:36.648 --> 00:06:39.773
He's been dead a decade. at least.

120
00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:44.357
Our timeline just jumped.

121
00:06:44.395 --> 00:06:45.321
I heard.

122
00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:48.196
Brings back bad memories in this city.

123
00:06:48.231 --> 00:06:52.861
'90 to '93. the average
murder rate was 2.000 per year.

124
00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.083
"Dream with me a city that can
be better than the way it is now."

125
00:06:56.118 --> 00:06:59.899
Inauguration speech from
Giuliani's first term. 1994.

126
00:06:59.938 --> 00:07:02.890
I was working Narcotics back
then out of Brooklyn North.

127
00:07:02.948 --> 00:07:05.591
We'd collared up every
day; barely made a dent.

128
00:07:05.606 --> 00:07:09.349
In the parks. you found more
crack vials and hypos than kids.

129
00:07:09.387 --> 00:07:11.394
It was a dangerous time.

130
00:07:11.432 --> 00:07:14.770
Here we go. We have six possible
matches to the body fingerprint.

131
00:07:14.808 --> 00:07:16.255
Who do you want to start with?

132
00:07:26.572 --> 00:07:27.247
With him.

133
00:07:29.432 --> 00:07:30.686
Lester Jayne.

134
00:08:01.653 --> 00:08:02.945
Stella.

135
00:08:16.698 --> 00:08:18.569
Admiring your work?

136
00:08:19.785 --> 00:08:20.711
Hey.

137
00:08:23.699 --> 00:08:26.632
Can't believe you're jamming me up for
this. I burned a few tourists. That's all.

138
00:08:26.651 --> 00:08:27.249
It was a joke.

139
00:08:27.345 --> 00:08:29.178
NYPD didn't find it funny.

140
00:08:29.198 --> 00:08:30.201
That's their problem.

141
00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.127
No. it's yours.

142
00:08:31.148 --> 00:08:34.447
Autopsy revealed the
skeleton's a homicide victim.

143
00:08:36.646 --> 00:08:37.650
Murder?

144
00:08:38.749 --> 00:08:40.370
No. I ain't buying that.

145
00:08:40.408 --> 00:08:42.434
Your little joke had a fractured skull.

146
00:08:42.451 --> 00:08:44.149
Which had nothing to do with me.

147
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:46.464
No? Then who does it have to do with?

148
00:08:46.483 --> 00:08:47.506
I know what you guys do.

149
00:08:47.525 --> 00:08:49.377
You twist things all around till
I don't know which end is up.

150
00:08:49.397 --> 00:08:53.718
I told you. all I did was screw with some
tourists. and I'm not saying another word.

151
00:08:56.866 --> 00:08:58.486
Where's he going?

152
00:11:11.113 --> 00:11:16.206
Decomposition on the
clothes and under the bones.

153
00:11:17.267 --> 00:11:18.946
I think this is where he died.

154
00:11:25.297 --> 00:11:27.631
I think it's also where he lived.

155
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.014
What do you think?

156
00:11:39.033 --> 00:11:39.862
Runaway?

157
00:11:40.017 --> 00:11:41.020
Maybe.

158
00:11:46.513 --> 00:11:47.671
"A.M."

159
00:11:47.729 --> 00:11:48.906
Initials?

160
00:11:48.963 --> 00:11:49.774
Yeah

161
00:11:53.783 --> 00:11:56.233
Home must've been pretty
bad to trade for this place.

162
00:11:56.291 --> 00:11:59.011
I'm sure this wasn't
what he had in mind.

163
00:12:00.979 --> 00:12:03.178
Skull fracture.

164
00:12:04.737 --> 00:12:06.994
Blunt force trauma?

165
00:12:17.483 --> 00:12:18.814
Pocketknife.

166
00:12:22.669 --> 00:12:25.929
Must have been pretty important
to him to keep it in his shoe.

167
00:12:33.796 --> 00:12:36.284
"Bright Lights. Big City."

168
00:12:37.866 --> 00:12:39.950
Pages are dog-eared.

169
00:12:39.994 --> 00:12:41.653
Highlighted.

170
00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.460
Copyright 1984.

171
00:12:48.097 --> 00:12:51.125
He may have been lying
here for 20 years.

172
00:12:51.745 --> 00:12:53.597
Have you ever read it?

173
00:12:54.311 --> 00:12:55.777
No. You?

174
00:12:55.796 --> 00:12:56.510
Yeah.

175
00:12:56.549 --> 00:12:58.883
It's about a guy who lives up
New York for all it's worth.

176
00:12:58.902 --> 00:13:00.484
How's it end?

177
00:13:01.155 --> 00:13:03.798
He gets out before the city kills him.

178
00:14:33.121 --> 00:14:35.127
It's tough being in the bullpen. huh?

179
00:14:35.166 --> 00:14:36.536
Waiting for the nod.

180
00:14:36.573 --> 00:14:38.251
Thanks for your concern.

181
00:14:42.013 --> 00:14:43.344
Here we go.

182
00:15:08.522 --> 00:15:09.891
Very nice.

183
00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:30.916
Got a full skeleton now.

184
00:15:30.955 --> 00:15:33.598
No more speculation.
Facts from here on out.

185
00:15:33.984 --> 00:15:35.450
Femur gives stature.

186
00:15:35.508 --> 00:15:37.167
He was
five-foot-ten.

187
00:15:37.228 --> 00:15:39.909
Mandible completes the skull.

188
00:15:39.967 --> 00:15:41.453
Check out his teeth.

189
00:15:42.494 --> 00:15:43.613
Nice.

190
00:15:43.691 --> 00:15:44.810
Too nice.

191
00:15:44.867 --> 00:15:45.697
Braces?

192
00:15:45.736 --> 00:15:46.970
Had to be.

193
00:15:47.044 --> 00:15:50.555
Two canine teeth. two second
bicuspids. but no first bicuspids.

194
00:15:50.575 --> 00:15:52.909
Caucasians commonly suffer
from dental crowding.

195
00:15:52.928 --> 00:15:55.475
Teeth were pulled to make room
for the others to straighten out.

196
00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:58.789
Okay. So. at some point in his life
he saw a dentist and an orthodontist.

197
00:15:58.828 --> 00:16:01.259
Which means he probably had
parents who paid the bill.

198
00:16:01.278 --> 00:16:04.037
It was probably the last
time he got to see a doctor.

199
00:16:04.076 --> 00:16:06.256
At least. that's what
his tibia's saying.

200
00:16:09.882 --> 00:16:10.789
Broke his leg.

201
00:16:10.827 --> 00:16:12.351
And let it heal on its own.

202
00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:14.338
Probably left him with a solid limp.

203
00:16:14.396 --> 00:16:17.001
This kid was moving
in the wrong direction.

204
00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.094
Checked him head to toe.

205
00:16:19.133 --> 00:16:23.184
With what I've got. he only potential
fatal injury is a skull fracture.

206
00:16:23.204 --> 00:16:25.885
Danny's running prints on a
pipe we found at the scene.

207
00:16:25.924 --> 00:16:26.869
Could that have done the trick?

208
00:16:26.888 --> 00:16:27.544
Easily.

209
00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:28.323
He ID'd?

210
00:16:28.362 --> 00:16:30.464
Sent out dentals
results aren't back yet.

211
00:16:30.542 --> 00:16:33.513
I'll cut a section of
mid-shaft femur. send it to DNA.

212
00:16:33.551 --> 00:16:36.638
But if he's a street kid.
I wouldn't expect much.

213
00:16:36.696 --> 00:16:38.259
I got a back-up plan.

214
00:16:38.302 --> 00:16:40.559
Been waiting all day
for this. Talk to me.

215
00:16:40.617 --> 00:16:42.257
White. John Doe teenager.

216
00:16:42.296 --> 00:16:44.109
Nice teeth. Bum leg.

217
00:16:44.186 --> 00:16:45.305
No face.

218
00:16:45.344 --> 00:16:46.598
No problem.

219
00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:33.180
Here he is.

220
00:19:34.395 --> 00:19:36.575
John Doe teenager.

221
00:19:49.004 --> 00:19:51.820
Fingerprints Danny lifted went nowhere.

222
00:19:52.183 --> 00:19:56.601
Whoever left them...
isn't listed in AFIS.

223
00:19:56.659 --> 00:19:57.508
Do you have a name?

224
00:19:57.546 --> 00:19:58.414
No.

225
00:19:58.565 --> 00:20:04.565
Got usable DNA off the bones. but there are no
matches in the CODIS Missing Persons Database.

226
00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.169
We can't ID him on DNA.

227
00:20:12.663 --> 00:20:15.113
Mac... you still with me?

228
00:20:15.152 --> 00:20:16.753
Yeah. Yeah. Sorry.

229
00:20:17.386 --> 00:20:20.974
I was just thinking about
something you said earlier...

230
00:20:21.013 --> 00:20:26.820
about a guy following his dreams...
getting out before the city kills him.

231
00:20:29.173 --> 00:20:31.411
He never got out. Why?

232
00:20:32.916 --> 00:20:35.270
Hey... Danny just called.

233
00:20:35.324 --> 00:20:38.604
Says there's some people who
want to see you... about him.

234
00:20:42.493 --> 00:20:49.574
We. uh. we waited for this day so
long... Finding Aaron... Our son.

235
00:20:49.608 --> 00:20:53.505
Mr. Moreland. we haven't been able to
positively identify the remains that were found.

236
00:20:53.521 --> 00:21:00.930
We read in the papers. and the detective at DCPI
confirmed. that a backpack and a pocketknife were found.

237
00:21:00.987 --> 00:21:02.647
Uh... uh...they belonged to Aaron.

238
00:21:02.681 --> 00:21:04.591
The backpack was monogrammed.

239
00:21:04.629 --> 00:21:06.906
I stitched it myself.

240
00:21:06.945 --> 00:21:11.440
And the pocketknife was a
gift for his 13th birthday.

241
00:21:11.474 --> 00:21:13.944
I ordered it special.

242
00:21:14.503 --> 00:21:17.493
We found other items. also.

243
00:21:17.513 --> 00:21:20.542
Clothes... a book.

244
00:21:42.762 --> 00:21:44.460
It's Aaron.

245
00:21:46.061 --> 00:21:48.762
Was Aaron an artist?

246
00:21:48.801 --> 00:21:49.900
Yes.

247
00:21:49.977 --> 00:21:51.830
A musician.

248
00:21:54.032 --> 00:21:58.913
Uh... Aaron. uh... he left home in 1987.

249
00:21:59.569 --> 00:22:03.215
Uh... he was 17. just
out of high school.

250
00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:07.764
He wanted to see something other than
Minnesota before thinking about college.

251
00:22:07.822 --> 00:22:13.166
This is a reconstruction of what
the skeleton would look like.

252
00:22:31.717 --> 00:22:35.923
It's. um... it's not Aaron.

253
00:22:36.579 --> 00:22:41.402
That's... not our son.

254
00:22:42.574 --> 00:22:45.005
I
- I don't know who that is.

255
00:22:53.973 --> 00:22:55.324
I'm sorry.

256
00:23:04.837 --> 00:23:05.416
Nice.

257
00:23:05.474 --> 00:23:07.075
Just the person I was looking for.

258
00:23:07.114 --> 00:23:09.950
I put some of my time into that
fingerprint I pulled from the pipe.

259
00:23:09.988 --> 00:23:11.532
Extra effort. I like that. Talk to me.

260
00:23:11.547 --> 00:23:17.161
I got 117 arrest records for people charged with crimes that were
committed within three blocks of where our skeleton was found.

261
00:23:17.181 --> 00:23:19.341
Time frame
- 1984 to '94.

262
00:23:19.361 --> 00:23:20.653
You used the CARS system.

263
00:23:20.688 --> 00:23:22.714
Yeah. where suspects are acquitted
or the charges are dropped.

264
00:23:22.733 --> 00:23:25.261
Which means the fingerprints
won't be found in AFIS.

265
00:23:25.299 --> 00:23:27.769
They're somewhere in a
sealed file of the NYPD.

266
00:23:27.807 --> 00:23:29.023
Mm-hmm. I just got to find them.

267
00:23:29.115 --> 00:23:31.874
And hope that they match the
prints you pulled off the pipe.

268
00:23:32.105 --> 00:23:33.263
That's a long shot. Danny.

269
00:23:33.282 --> 00:23:34.035
What're you saying?

270
00:23:34.073 --> 00:23:35.192
You want me to drop this lead?

271
00:23:35.231 --> 00:23:37.700
No. I want you to stick
to the hot evidence first.

272
00:23:37.735 --> 00:23:39.568
The clothes the victim was
found in. I'm already on it.

273
00:23:39.626 --> 00:23:42.250
Good. all right? You
learn something. find me.

274
00:23:42.269 --> 00:23:45.433
You come up dry. get back on
those arrest records. Got it?

275
00:23:45.472 --> 00:23:46.128
Got it.

276
00:24:27.389 --> 00:24:28.547
Hi. Playing hangman?

277
00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:31.441
Actually. yes.

278
00:24:32.079 --> 00:24:34.240
But I've run out of letters.

279
00:24:34.298 --> 00:24:36.902
I have... I have no idea what this is.

280
00:24:37.558 --> 00:24:39.005
For now.

281
00:24:49.038 --> 00:24:51.257
What do you make of this?

282
00:24:53.244 --> 00:24:54.807
Look familiar?

283
00:24:56.678 --> 00:24:57.951
Not really.

284
00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:02.138
It's the last sketch in his book. and...
looks like it could be an island...

285
00:25:02.172 --> 00:25:06.397
...but not in New York.
No people. no buildings.

286
00:25:06.419 --> 00:25:13.441
Got a call from Missing Persons. They pulled all the Unsolved
Case folders from teenage boys missing within our time frame.

287
00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:16.219
Thumbed through them by hand.
based on Aiden's reconstruction.

288
00:25:16.277 --> 00:25:17.203
No matches.

289
00:25:17.276 --> 00:25:20.112
Unlike Aaron Moreland. there's no
one out there looking for this kid.

290
00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:24.916
So all we know about who he
was. are his images of New York.

291
00:25:24.993 --> 00:25:26.440
Skyline.

292
00:25:26.842 --> 00:25:29.504
Busy Grand Central Station.

293
00:25:29.543 --> 00:25:31.144
Empire State Building.

294
00:25:31.182 --> 00:25:33.035
See his perspective?

295
00:25:33.073 --> 00:25:34.539
Street level.

296
00:25:35.133 --> 00:25:37.603
Part of the city. moving with the flow.

297
00:25:37.661 --> 00:25:40.613
Yeah. but look at this one.

298
00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:45.108
He's totally alone in Times Square.

299
00:25:45.269 --> 00:25:47.970
He was swallowed up by the city.

300
00:25:49.301 --> 00:25:50.633
Lost.

301
00:25:50.671 --> 00:25:55.610
Reality rarely lives up to expectations.
especially if you're a teenager.

302
00:25:57.295 --> 00:25:59.803
This one with the crisscross
beams. That's the Port Authority.

303
00:25:59.861 --> 00:26:01.365
Upper parking deck.

304
00:26:01.385 --> 00:26:03.661
Yeah. but he's looking
at it straight on.

305
00:26:03.719 --> 00:26:05.610
Which means...

306
00:26:06.837 --> 00:26:12.239
He's inside the only building in the city that
allows homeless kids free reign to every floor.

307
00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:19.200
I started Danner House in a tenement
on the Lower East Side in 1969.

308
00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:21.785
Intended it to be a youth crisis center.

309
00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:25.177
Nobody under the age of
21 would be turned away.

310
00:26:25.293 --> 00:26:28.727
First month. 500 kids
came through the door.

311
00:26:28.746 --> 00:26:31.312
5.000 by the end of the first year.

312
00:26:31.502 --> 00:26:34.241
There's never a shortage
of people that need help.

313
00:26:42.110 --> 00:26:44.791
Guess we're not an
uncommon sight around here.

314
00:26:45.389 --> 00:26:47.242
Joel. how we doing today?

315
00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:49.248
Uh... We're running low.

316
00:26:49.287 --> 00:26:50.811
I don't think we're going to make it.

317
00:26:50.846 --> 00:26:54.569
Call over to St. Juliana's. See if they
have any meals left over from yesterday.

318
00:26:54.743 --> 00:26:57.212
They usually run out
of meals before we do.

319
00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:00.164
Would you by chance
recognize this young man?

320
00:27:00.638 --> 00:27:05.847
We don't have a name for him. but we think he might
have stayed here sometime between 1984 and 1994.

321
00:27:05.886 --> 00:27:07.969
It's been a long time.

322
00:27:08.992 --> 00:27:12.175
I've been here 35 years.

323
00:27:12.504 --> 00:27:17.038
I used to try to get
to know the kids. but...

324
00:27:17.482 --> 00:27:21.147
After a while. all the
faces start to look the same.

325
00:28:35.173 --> 00:28:37.854
I'm going to check with Danny.
see how he's doing on the clothes.

326
00:28:37.889 --> 00:28:40.803
You going to follow up with the
piece of paper we found in the jeans?

327
00:28:41.478 --> 00:28:43.465
Mr. and Mrs. Moreland?

328
00:28:44.179 --> 00:28:47.613
Do you know who the boy
was that had Aaron's things?

329
00:28:47.659 --> 00:28:53.929
No. No. unfortunately we don't know anything new
that you that you haven't already read in the papers.

330
00:28:53.967 --> 00:28:58.192
But he-he had Aaron's
backpack an-and pocketknife.

331
00:28:58.228 --> 00:28:59.655
How did he get them?

332
00:28:59.694 --> 00:29:00.678
We don't know.

333
00:29:00.697 --> 00:29:04.382
But they're the only
connection to my son.

334
00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:07.700
They had to know each other. right?

335
00:29:07.716 --> 00:29:09.858
I need to know what happened.

336
00:29:09.896 --> 00:29:12.597
I know the reality...

337
00:29:12.636 --> 00:29:15.838
He's probably dead...
I've accepted that. but...

338
00:29:15.854 --> 00:29:21.294
but this is the closest that we've.
we've come to the truth in 17 years.

339
00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:23.204
Please.

340
00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:26.619
What happened to my boy?

341
00:29:33.433 --> 00:29:39.163
He's somebody else's case. isn't he?

342
00:29:43.187 --> 00:29:51.483
You don't know what... what happened to
Aaron... because you're not looking for him...

343
00:29:52.215 --> 00:29:53.585
Are you?

344
00:29:56.749 --> 00:29:57.887
No.

345
00:30:28.551 --> 00:30:29.747
Mac?

346
00:30:29.786 --> 00:30:33.721
I took some new photos of the reconstruction.
I thought I'd send them out nationally.

347
00:30:33.753 --> 00:30:36.281
And maybe someone will recognize him.

348
00:30:36.319 --> 00:30:42.397
Work One Police Plaza. Use the in-house reporters
from the major papers. Get his face all over the city.

349
00:30:43.318 --> 00:30:45.132
You've already done that. haven't you?

350
00:30:45.151 --> 00:30:49.280
I figured it was one of the few times that
us and the media are on the same team. so...

351
00:30:49.318 --> 00:30:52.000
Well. stay on them. Keep him front page.

352
00:30:53.352 --> 00:30:56.053
Hey. do you need anything else?

353
00:30:57.288 --> 00:30:58.253
Yeah.

354
00:30:59.237 --> 00:31:01.397
I need to know what this is.

355
00:31:32.824 --> 00:31:37.088
Anyway. most of the shirt was gone. but I
can make out a small portion of the logo.

356
00:31:37.126 --> 00:31:38.342
An "L." Lagaronne.

357
00:31:38.380 --> 00:31:40.039
Fancy. and expensive.

358
00:31:40.059 --> 00:31:43.338
And synthetic. Nylon microfiber.
All the rage in the early '90s.

359
00:31:43.377 --> 00:31:45.905
The victim had a couple of things
on him that didn't belong to him.

360
00:31:45.924 --> 00:31:47.467
Same probably goes for the shirt.

361
00:31:47.506 --> 00:31:49.281
You know. five-finger discount.

362
00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.191
Yeah. but not from a store.

363
00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:52.001
What do you know?

364
00:31:52.036 --> 00:31:55.856
I reached out to the manufacturer. Turns
out that shirt never went on the market.

365
00:31:55.895 --> 00:31:58.056
Prototype from 1988.

366
00:31:58.133 --> 00:32:01.181
All right. well. if there was no store
to steal it from. how'd he get it?

367
00:32:01.216 --> 00:32:03.358
Seventh Avenue. right off the rack.

368
00:32:16.759 --> 00:32:17.801
Rack runner.

369
00:32:17.820 --> 00:32:21.717
No experience necessary. If you can walk
from point "A" to point "B." you're hired.

370
00:32:21.747 --> 00:32:26.397
It's a perfect job for a teenager. and
easy access to clothes you can't afford.

371
00:32:31.505 --> 00:32:36.039
So management's official statement is that they
unofficially hire street kids to run the racks.

372
00:32:36.077 --> 00:32:38.450
In other words. cheap
labor. no paperwork.

373
00:32:38.489 --> 00:32:40.187
Did anyone recognize the vic?

374
00:32:40.225 --> 00:32:41.904
Life span with the racks isn't too long.

375
00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.136
Here today. gone tomorrow.

376
00:32:43.175 --> 00:32:45.683
There's nobody working here now
that was working here in '88.

377
00:32:45.702 --> 00:32:46.590
Of course not.

378
00:32:46.609 --> 00:32:49.194
Time marches on. the
practice stays the same.

379
00:32:58.739 --> 00:33:00.379
Dead end on the shirt.

380
00:33:00.398 --> 00:33:02.346
But it wasn't a total loss.

381
00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:06.394
Since it wasn't made till
1988. we know he was alive then.

382
00:33:06.433 --> 00:33:08.613
So we've narrowed our time line.

383
00:33:08.632 --> 00:33:11.294
Yeah. six-year window instead of ten.

384
00:33:12.834 --> 00:33:14.338
Make that four.

385
00:33:14.396 --> 00:33:17.908
He was alive February 21. 1990.

386
00:33:17.946 --> 00:33:19.490
Pawned a watch.

387
00:33:19.563 --> 00:33:19.968
Yeah.

388
00:33:20.759 --> 00:33:22.553
And we have the ticket.

389
00:33:23.865 --> 00:33:26.894
I'm sorry. pal. but
you're a few hours late.

390
00:33:26.933 --> 00:33:28.630
Owner just came in and picked it up.

391
00:33:28.665 --> 00:33:31.482
The owner's been dead
since Dinkins was mayor.

392
00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:32.736
He didn't just come by.

393
00:33:32.774 --> 00:33:34.723
Look. what do you want me to tell you?

394
00:33:34.762 --> 00:33:36.112
I had that watch forever.

395
00:33:36.147 --> 00:33:40.025
Today a guy comes in. describes it
perfectly. Even the inscription on the back.

396
00:33:40.064 --> 00:33:42.726
Ticket or no ticket.
the thing had to be his.

397
00:33:42.765 --> 00:33:46.912
No coincidence the watch is
picked up today after 14 years.

398
00:33:47.168 --> 00:33:50.062
Somebody else knew it was here
and didn't want us to get it first.

399
00:33:50.101 --> 00:33:52.628
Someone who knows we're looking for him.

400
00:33:53.974 --> 00:33:58.971
But it's not 1990 anymore. It's
gotten a lot harder to hide.

401
00:34:01.591 --> 00:34:03.462
Guy played the camera perfectly.

402
00:34:03.482 --> 00:34:05.507
Kept his head down the entire time.

403
00:34:05.604 --> 00:34:08.536
So this is our best view
of the only suspect we have?

404
00:34:08.575 --> 00:34:09.945
Unfortunately.

405
00:34:09.992 --> 00:34:11.285
Get creative.

406
00:34:12.018 --> 00:34:12.963
Done.

407
00:34:13.407 --> 00:34:14.275
Okay...

408
00:34:15.471 --> 00:34:17.130
From the top.

409
00:34:19.037 --> 00:34:20.985
Mystery man enters.

410
00:34:21.043 --> 00:34:23.107
Keeps his head down. goes
straight to the counter.

411
00:34:24.265 --> 00:34:26.310
Didn't even stop to look at the jewelry.

412
00:34:26.345 --> 00:34:28.872
He doesn't have to.
he knows what he wants.

413
00:34:31.168 --> 00:34:31.998
Freeze it.

414
00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:35.084
Bruno's glasses. Can
you pull a reflection?

415
00:34:35.123 --> 00:34:36.763
Mm-hmm. No problem.

416
00:34:39.231 --> 00:34:42.144
Cleaning up. we have...

417
00:34:42.414 --> 00:34:44.614
A great shot of his baseball cap.

418
00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:46.099
Keep going.

419
00:34:54.332 --> 00:34:57.129
He's steps away from
freedom. What's he doing?

420
00:34:58.688 --> 00:35:01.177
It's not on screen. but that
doesn't mean it's not on tape.

421
00:35:01.216 --> 00:35:03.724
NTSC signals are overscanned.

422
00:35:03.762 --> 00:35:05.749
All right. how much are we missing?

423
00:35:12.141 --> 00:35:13.183
It's a guitar...

424
00:35:13.222 --> 00:35:14.167
It's DNA...

425
00:35:19.887 --> 00:35:21.353
Creative enough for you?

426
00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:04.725
Partial DNA profile from the guitar strings has
got a hit in the CODIS Missing Persons database.

427
00:36:07.499 --> 00:36:10.952
The missing kid who owned
the backpack and pocketknife?

428
00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:14.174
Aaron Moreland... You're kidding?

429
00:36:14.415 --> 00:36:16.344
He disappeared in '87.

430
00:36:16.383 --> 00:36:18.003
He was 17 years old.

431
00:36:18.023 --> 00:36:20.087
Which means he's now 34.

432
00:36:20.145 --> 00:36:22.113
And the prime suspect in a murder.

433
00:36:22.146 --> 00:36:23.323
Where are we?

434
00:36:23.535 --> 00:36:25.059
We're there.

435
00:36:25.097 --> 00:36:28.397
In 1987. Aaron Moreland
was a 17-year-old kid.

436
00:36:28.416 --> 00:36:32.332
Here's a pretty good idea
of what he looks like today.

437
00:36:42.344 --> 00:36:44.794
You guys know him?

438
00:36:44.833 --> 00:36:48.517
It was your DNA on the guitar string.

439
00:36:50.385 --> 00:36:52.334
I was going to be a musician. you know.

440
00:36:52.372 --> 00:36:54.938
That's why I came to New York.

441
00:36:54.996 --> 00:36:56.617
To be a rock star.

442
00:36:56.906 --> 00:36:59.125
But I was 17.

443
00:36:59.198 --> 00:37:04.870
Had spent every day of my life
in a town with one intersection.

444
00:37:05.753 --> 00:37:07.702
I wasn't prepared for this city.

445
00:37:09.072 --> 00:37:13.509
By the end of my first week
here. I was broke... and hooked.

446
00:37:13.953 --> 00:37:15.573
Did you know him?

447
00:37:18.389 --> 00:37:20.087
Sort of.

448
00:37:20.241 --> 00:37:24.427
I used to ran racks down on Seventh Avenue
and I'd see him there every now and then.

449
00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:31.088
He always had money
and I always needed it.

450
00:37:34.175 --> 00:37:35.719
How did you get the pocketknife?

451
00:37:35.754 --> 00:37:39.169
When I was high. I'd
get angry very easily.

452
00:37:39.188 --> 00:37:42.005
And so I didn't want to do
anything that I would regret later.

453
00:37:42.024 --> 00:37:43.972
So you also sold the knife?

454
00:37:43.992 --> 00:37:45.651
Yeah. He was easy to find.

455
00:37:53.763 --> 00:37:54.785
Port Authority...

456
00:37:54.824 --> 00:37:55.846
all those busses.

457
00:37:55.900 --> 00:37:57.617
All you had to do was get on one.

458
00:37:58.794 --> 00:38:00.144
I didn't want to go home.

459
00:38:01.051 --> 00:38:01.803
Here you go. man.

460
00:38:02.826 --> 00:38:04.254
It's a pawn ticket.

461
00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:05.932
For my watch.

462
00:38:11.478 --> 00:38:14.854
Man. that's all you got?! I need more.

463
00:38:17.531 --> 00:38:20.367
You killed him over two dollars?

464
00:38:21.254 --> 00:38:23.434
Six years later. I got clean.

465
00:38:23.492 --> 00:38:24.978
Changed my life.

466
00:38:25.016 --> 00:38:26.213
My name.

467
00:38:26.328 --> 00:38:27.930
Began helping others.

468
00:38:27.953 --> 00:38:30.017
And forgot about your past.

469
00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:33.644
Until I picked up the
paper and it all came back.

470
00:38:33.702 --> 00:38:35.149
So you tried to cover it up.

471
00:38:35.303 --> 00:38:39.085
You're not in the system. Your own parents
thought you were dead. Everything was good.

472
00:38:39.479 --> 00:38:43.472
But you knew there was still one thing
out there that connected you to the victim.

473
00:38:43.530 --> 00:38:44.823
Your watch.

474
00:38:49.371 --> 00:38:55.545
You may have started over. but your mother
and father never stopped looking for you.

475
00:38:59.616 --> 00:39:00.542
Get up.

476
00:39:09.914 --> 00:39:10.975
Aaron...

477
00:39:11.851 --> 00:39:13.761
What was his name?

478
00:39:16.597 --> 00:39:18.334
I don't know.

479
00:40:15.517 --> 00:40:17.234
You already know how it ends.

480
00:40:19.337 --> 00:40:21.806
That's not why I'm reading it.

481
00:40:25.545 --> 00:40:32.799
"But what you are left with is a premonition of the way your life
will fade behind you. like a book you have read too quickly...

482
00:40:32.901 --> 00:40:39.615
leaving a dwindling trail of images and
emotions. until all you can remember is a name."

483
00:40:39.692 --> 00:40:41.004
His name.

484
00:40:41.946 --> 00:40:43.875
It's the only thing we don't know.

485
00:40:49.412 --> 00:40:51.631
Now we never will.

486
00:41:00.315 --> 00:41:02.707
So that's it. huh? Case closed.

487
00:41:04.246 --> 00:41:05.114
Yep.

488
00:41:07.815 --> 00:41:12.098
Listen. a bunch of us are going to
Sullivan's for a drink. Want to come with?

489
00:41:12.576 --> 00:41:15.836
Nah. I got case files to the moon.

490
00:41:15.855 --> 00:41:18.093
Case files can wait. Mac.

491
00:41:18.132 --> 00:41:20.428
Come on. you need to get out.

492
00:41:20.501 --> 00:41:24.707
If this case taught us anything.
it was to live life. right?

493
00:41:25.247 --> 00:41:26.926
Thanks anyway. Next time.

494
00:41:27.504 --> 00:41:29.974
All right. Suit yourself.

495
00:41:32.594 --> 00:41:33.656
Hey. Stella...

496
00:41:33.984 --> 00:41:35.083
Yeah?

497
00:41:36.607 --> 00:41:38.961
How's the coffee there?

498
00:41:39.092 --> 00:41:42.680
They have Irish coffee.
You're gonna love it.
